        ":db/id": 17592186096451,
        ":question/id": "dccb5bac-e528-5b42-9949-36b3870fdde1",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "e-4287",
        ":question/problem": "Consider the picture below. Given that $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n![](images/b8/b8abf22d14f93a38c4d01f368b8171e4ccfe90086d932f5b58453baa46391405.png)",
        ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/76/76b9867a7c1ef484922241ebe44272f0104e406c31310bf36227a13850f50d8b.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup ONM$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $ON=2|\\mathbf{n}|$;\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{ON}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{ON}=2\\mathbf{n}$.\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $NM=2|\\mathbf{k}|$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{NM}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{NM}=2\\mathbf{k}$.\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{m} & =\\overset{\\to }{ON}+\\overset{\\to }{NM} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{n}+2\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}=2\\mathbf{n}+2\\mathbf{k}$. \n\nSince $\\mathbf{n} \\nparallel \\mathbf{k},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{n}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda=2$ and $\\mu=2$"
      },
      ":knowledge-point/id": "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0",
