          ":question/difficulty": {
            ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
          },
          ":question/id": "1b7aee39-22ab-5444-970d-797a67e85565",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-19370",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/a1/a1e251e00a99520cc392d031dbd05646ce07b65319d829e2cd8fc6f80e6e29a2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mu \\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/60/601e049067de307ef2321ae9b516d1c7306d837ca0758c080074446a8c54389d.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=2|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=2\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=2|\\mathbf{b}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mu \\mathbf{b}=2\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =2$"
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186097241,
