          },
          ":question/id": "a6df187f-d344-54eb-95de-18be02ffa7f9",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-42670",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/01/01940c9fa894d635300a49867da35d483b873a45d2a75204c20c58e4cb84c883.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{t}=\\lambda \\mathbf{r}+\\mu \\mathbf{s}$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/a0/a0d9fa1c366945e5e789b6361f929357af163db2e9be4a3ccc9023c12486aca6.png)\n\nIn $△ORT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OR=2|\\mathbf{r}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{r}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=-2\\mathbf{r}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $RT=2|\\mathbf{s}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{s}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=-2\\mathbf{s}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t} & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RT} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{r}-2\\mathbf{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{t}=\\lambda \\mathbf{r}+\\mu \\mathbf{s}=-2\\mathbf{r}-2\\mathbf{s}.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{s}∦\\mathbf{r},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-2$"
        },
