          ":question/problem": "![](images/25/25717d521feeec25f7b4b722ca7b8a4ee9b5fdc8423bac06953e5f2adc8eef3d.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{t}=\\lambda \\mathbf{r}+\\mu \\mathbf{s}$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/d5/d585c949d7de8cc3fa2d62bfb60990af12242c32ef3390e563f7e627844c5a88.png)\n\nIn $△ORT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OR=3|\\mathbf{r}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ and $\\mathbf{r}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=3\\mathbf{r}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $RT=2|\\mathbf{s}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{s}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=-2\\mathbf{s}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t} & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RT} \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{r}-2\\mathbf{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{t}=\\lambda \\mathbf{r}+\\mu \\mathbf{s}=3\\mathbf{r}-2\\mathbf{s}.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{s}∦\\mathbf{r},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =-2$"
        },
