          ":question/id": "15e9fa4b-b469-5d54-bb3e-fd8c74e844aa",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-120277",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/c9/c991e8d6cc9cb869db0a0a663830f255f33dd32756167a5e665b2e4318ae7014.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/a9/a9dcb3e3cd825cf24103f3636b79418531f8b8a14a5f6c83b9b94780fdf8739b.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=4|\\mathbf{p}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=4\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|\\mathbf{q}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{q}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=-2\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =4\\mathbf{p}-2\\mathbf{q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}=4\\mathbf{p}-2\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{p}∦\\mathbf{q},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =4$ and $\\mu =-2$"
        },
