          ":question/id": "281f178c-d09e-5385-8ce5-3504549094a9",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-120279",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/fc/fc7d37da99173b7fa99f2f5f0bbf1478a54505628ed827488a18b3d01535f81f.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{l}=\\lambda \\mathbf{j}+\\mu \\mathbf{k}$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/6c/6c9273c0b89d4c06c8a8591b8e8e94b0c83b2d1d4bd04b64ed8cb0a185619b5f.png)\n\nIn $△OJL$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OJ=|\\mathbf{j}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{j}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=-\\mathbf{j}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $JL=2|\\mathbf{k}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{k}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{JL}=-2\\mathbf{k}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{l} & =\\overset{\\to }{OJ}+\\overset{\\to }{JL} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{l}=\\lambda \\mathbf{j}+\\mu \\mathbf{k}=-\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{j}∦\\mathbf{k},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$"
        },
