          ":question/difficulty": {
            ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
          },
          ":question/id": "d447ec6b-433a-5b79-9c9d-143f856c643d",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-275128",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/99/992be4e67316804381ffc38860d2eaf23fd4a93cfe22ec4918c30c41e34ade57.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{t}=\\lambda \\mathbf{r}+\\mu \\mathbf{s}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n\n$-\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nand \n$\\mu =$\n\n$-\\frac{5}{2}$\n\n.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/c0/c0f7f4df52373ca602aff41c97a5b8413f510353874540b76147c527a2f4b18c.png)\n\nIn $△ORT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OR=\\frac{1}{2}|\\mathbf{r}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{r}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{r}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $RT=\\frac{5}{2}|\\mathbf{s}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{s}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=-\\frac{5}{2}\\mathbf{s}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t} & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RT} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{r}-\\frac{5}{2}\\mathbf{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{t}=\\lambda \\mathbf{r}+\\mu \\mathbf{s}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{r}-\\frac{5}{2}\\mathbf{s}.\n$$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{s}∦\\mathbf{r},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{5}{2}$"
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186302254,
          ":question/answer-fields": [
            {
              ":answer-field/choices": [
                {
                  ":answer/id": "0ca2060e-cf6d-5017-97b3-a1a9cc42949a",
