          ":question/id": "8fc6062b-82cc-5523-a4f6-6f883f485c18",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-326013",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/58/586fb01cd796c9d4e04aaeb393ea7f5fadd6c836d0b39d36c9c668447c490bd1.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{t}=\\lambda \\mathbf{u}+\\mu \\mathbf{v}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/2d/2d3a5c31af776cc49c2af118edc7256ca533320c7d53245593e5de6649ce03cf.png)\n\nIn $△OXT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OX=|\\mathbf{u}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OX}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{u}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=-\\mathbf{u}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $XT=2|\\mathbf{v}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{XT}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{v}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-2\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-\\mathbf{u}-2\\mathbf{v}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{t}=\\lambda \\mathbf{u}+\\mu \\mathbf{v}=-1\\mathbf{u}-2\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{v}∦\\mathbf{u},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$"
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186302255,
          ":question/answer-fields": [
            {
              ":answer-field/choices": [
