          ":question/difficulty": {
            ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
          },
          ":question/id": "90a7567f-3548-5a15-bedd-9db11178758a",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-326014",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/de/de5905301a41f02237a48257a79e457f11ad74186942ca5bcf0002ab041aff91.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{t}=\\lambda \\mathbf{j}+\\mu \\mathbf{k}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/1b/1be50e101b386f53ea5a2f1474f2e1d2debb8f98380d49a346dda32b37db70f1.png)\n\nIn $△OXT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OX=2|\\mathbf{j}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OX}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{j}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=-2\\mathbf{j}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $XT=\\frac{1}{2}|\\mathbf{k}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{XT}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{k}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{k}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-2\\mathbf{j}-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{t}=\\lambda \\mathbf{j}+\\mu \\mathbf{k}=-2\\mathbf{j}-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{k}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{k}∦\\mathbf{j},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{1}{2}$"
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186302256,
          ":question/answer-fields": [
            {
              ":answer-field/choices": [
