          ":question/id": "d2bbedc4-0ff6-53bb-b9ae-7bd7218983f9",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-326022",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/99/99d63eb31fba0757bde11dda3653e3791fcddb0b74b5325e20afcede4f3ea849.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mu \\mathbf{b}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/f7/f7acd51dcca9ad48f4aa7ec026beaeb67ddc9b1e88ab9b5042016674ba6ea542.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=4|\\mathbf{b}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=4\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{a}+4\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mu \\mathbf{b}=1\\mathbf{a}+4\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =4$"
        },
