          ":question/id": "1beeeaab-084b-5797-a939-fcb45aee542f",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-327074",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/c8/c8cbd5b93633e4e4899a1cde0da618f3be792bdbbd77db650c32814187b222f8.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/67/6708537b79154a7778fe52e8d82b6577f93d40a06d3af0267e3e4b7eaa6468a9.png)\n\nIn $△OMR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OM=3|\\mathbf{q}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OM}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=-3\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $MR=2|\\mathbf{p}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{MR}$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{MR}=2\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MR} \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{q}+2\\mathbf{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}=2\\mathbf{p}-3\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{p}∦\\mathbf{q},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-3$"
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186302316,
          ":question/answer-fields": [
            {
              ":answer-field/choices": [
