          ":question/difficulty": {
            ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
          },
          ":question/id": "e61f5683-d5a7-570e-8d61-a9e450d0adae",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-327075",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/e6/e69402e97668c50f91258ca7e8dbb5206d1cd681e73bb9333303777adc866b70.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{i}=\\lambda \\mathbf{j}+\\mu \\mathbf{k}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/cb/cb8209592ec6d5be435622999c1b3eb86e1756cbe9852be0efdbbf41d41777ff.png)\n\nIn $△OJL$ shown above, we have  \n\n- $OJ=2|\\mathbf{j}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=-2\\mathbf{j}.$  \n\nSimilarly:  \n\n- $JL=|\\mathbf{k}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{JL}=\\mathbf{k}.$  \n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{i}=\\overset{\\to }{OJ}+\\overset{\\to }{JL}=-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{i}=\\lambda \\mathbf{j}+\\mu \\mathbf{k}=-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}.$  \n\nSince $\\mathbf{j}∦\\mathbf{k},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way.  Equating the coefficients, we get \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2,\\,\\mu =1.\n$$"
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186302317,
          ":question/answer-fields": [
            {
              ":answer-field/choices": [
