          ":question/difficulty": {
            ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
          },
          ":question/id": "c302ea94-ce6a-528e-80d0-db3b0e2f4650",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-327078",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/4c/4c1a8cde010abbeaf318a64e6c9f3b6cbd3e7bac84babbcda4c1ebfb483fa2b2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{s}=\\lambda \\mathbf{m}+\\mu \\mathbf{n}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/e0/e018cc5b4760b578d9ca514f9de6641e823bf5f6a8e17f019ebf24fc6ccb2248.png)\n\nIn $△OTS$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OT=4|\\mathbf{m}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OT}$ and $\\mathbf{m}$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OT}=-4\\mathbf{m}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $TS=2|\\mathbf{n}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{TS}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{TS}=2\\mathbf{n}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{s} & =\\overset{\\to }{OT}+\\overset{\\to }{TS} \\\\  & =-4\\mathbf{m}+2\\mathbf{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{s}=\\lambda \\mathbf{m}+\\mu \\mathbf{n}=-4\\mathbf{m}+2\\mathbf{n}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{m}∦\\mathbf{n},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-4$ and $\\mu =2$"
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186329675,
