          ":question/id": "4f6563bc-e431-538e-8c1d-8eaa7d0e394f",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-42657",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/dc/dc708da9adb4f61161ee1face89403bf1a6ce9762905e8a59b4b3ed9031c817a.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/84/84f8951c4921eca20b00132996dc7b2dfafe047d58574e1822da795e1444c861.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=3|\\mathbf{p}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{p}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=-3\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|\\mathbf{q}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{q}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=-2\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{p}-2\\mathbf{q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}=-3\\mathbf{p}-2\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{p}∦\\mathbf{q},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$"
        },
