            ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
          },
          ":question/id": "8c824c20-e7d8-5ee0-a1d9-a9f4762eb921",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-42662",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/e2/e2f149fb4f32fb264e0af6898e7737d570ec90bbff31cf1256eb9bd2636ceab3.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{w}=\\lambda \\mathbf{u}+\\mu \\mathbf{v}$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/58/589f9f524a7c98da61ad6c5001b8d392d89073d5734cd70cfa750d153fe6f7b7.png)\n\nIn $△OUW$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OU=2|\\mathbf{v}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OU}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{v}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=-2\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $UW=4|\\mathbf{u}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{UW}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{u}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{UW}=-4\\mathbf{u}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{w} & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{v}-4\\mathbf{u} \\\\  & =-4\\mathbf{u}-2\\mathbf{v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{w}=\\lambda \\mathbf{u}+\\mu \\mathbf{v}=-4\\mathbf{u}-2\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{u}∦\\mathbf{v},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-4$ and $\\mu =-2$"
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186329679,
          ":question/answer-fields": [
