          ":question/id": "ff56059b-4551-5fb8-813b-6274d8fd5efd",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-42676",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/be/be4bd3ff63baa05d861cbad6a26c6b53b8b7f1fed64feb0c0465408c6e7ced2d.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{l}=\\lambda \\mathbf{j}+\\mu \\mathbf{k}$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/a2/a216faf7db26da01730a18607a298e442af47a7d45a7a69e6fd12cbfd2229360.png)\n\nIn $△OJL$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OJ=3|\\mathbf{j}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=-3\\mathbf{j}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $JL=|\\mathbf{k}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{JL}=\\mathbf{k}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{l} & =\\overset{\\to }{OJ}+\\overset{\\to }{JL} \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{l}=\\lambda \\mathbf{j}+\\mu \\mathbf{k}=-3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{j}∦\\mathbf{k},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =1$"
        },
        {
