          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-77515",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/c8/c800644be3cc0ad0688d52948a4a2cb813cbe14b1fe899abe5a1d92613a687bf.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/90/90272fad8af526de6e169faba8daae29210c1f502c4959d0e1829e5ad65476f0.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=2|\\mathbf{p}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=2\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=4|\\mathbf{q}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=4\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{p}+4\\mathbf{q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}=2\\mathbf{p}+4\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{p}∦\\mathbf{q},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =4$"
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186365574,
          ":question/answer-fields": [
            {
              ":answer-field/choices": [
