          ":question/id": "b8cb8830-d33b-5725-abf1-a6d3f309ef27",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-77452",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/16/1649e6c4ef2874cda2a7d7428b54220ef2a1790ba4b197c755165c48add0c31e.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mu \\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/7c/7cf17440c27765efb34b6a01c12a76ad4e980fc540307e2218227153661db5dd.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=2.5|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=2.5\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=3|\\mathbf{b}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=3\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =2.5\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mu \\mathbf{b}=2.5\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n$\\,$ $\\lambda =2.5$ and $\\mu =3$"
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186420467,
          ":question/answer-fields": [
            {
              ":answer-field/choices": [
