          ":question/difficulty": {
            ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/hard"
          },
          ":question/id": "4fd40f19-a244-5f63-bc0a-cb69c15f6c44",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/df/df3c05b58a8b6831ee666a527e80a506ed2b6c0a7c7046849689381c8afe2dce.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/ea/ea6d05ad928486c371f9d38aa0c8a8e3edc671a2e64c0426557e454e11120705.png)\n\nIn $△OMR$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OM=\\frac{10}{3}|\\mathbf{q}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OM}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{q}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=-\\frac{10}{3}\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $MR=\\frac{5}{3}|\\mathbf{p}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{MR}$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{MR}=\\frac{5}{3}\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MR} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{3}\\mathbf{q}+\\frac{5}{3}\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\mathbf{p}-\\frac{10}{3}\\mathbf{q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}=\\frac{5}{3}\\mathbf{p}-\\frac{10}{3}\\mathbf{q}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{p}∦\\mathbf{q},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =\\frac{5}{3}$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{10}{3}$"
        },
        {
