          ":question/id": "68bf99a3-7fc9-5547-9120-f2c14067205b",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/7c/7c577ee7f4171d488642f9c8f9c8e4204269b05620ed22eae91db72b1f931837.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/01/0148dd6296370b01a9decbee10a96765be9af88c2f1eb8b9137d59131082c36f.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OP=|\\mathbf{p}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=|\\mathbf{q}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{p}∦\\mathbf{q},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =1$"
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186514769,
          ":question/answer-fields": [
            {
              ":answer-field/choices": [
