          ":question/difficulty": {
            ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/hard"
          },
          ":question/id": "b38e8849-a51d-558f-9b16-3471fb9b447e",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/ab/ab91b3b28b656d65dedc23b8ca71d386ae9f46b0ebbb2ab6b815fd7b81cfc2ba.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/a4/a4cb7a15f5cb1b818899d388b393da81d8fc873d2da5a1888286dbe8a9ffc4fd.png)\n\nIn $△OMR$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OM=\\frac{3}{2}|\\mathbf{q}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OM}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $MR=\\frac{11}{3}|\\mathbf{p}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{MR}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{p}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{MR}=-\\frac{11}{3}\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MR} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{q}-\\frac{11}{3}\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{3}\\mathbf{p}+\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}=-\\frac{11}{3}\\mathbf{p}+\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{q}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{p}∦\\mathbf{q},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{11}{3}$ and $\\mu =\\frac{3}{2}$"
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186514782,
          ":question/answer-fields": [
            {
              ":answer-field/choices": [
