          ":question/difficulty": {
            ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/hard"
          },
          ":question/id": "bbd1b0a1-ee60-5a86-a8ad-b5834136a338",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/2c/2c34e3e967bbad961e3bd82514479ebfcc391a6e66801aeb40d44c56b49c6968.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/cc/ccbb0fd7c3314645d1563a0b70a916b2008e3e6125242bbb9094d5de8da26950.png)\n\nIn $△OMR$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OM=\\frac{4}{3}|\\mathbf{q}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OM}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $MR=\\frac{15}{4}|\\mathbf{p}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{MR}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{p}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{MR}=-\\frac{15}{4}\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MR} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{q}-\\frac{15}{4}\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{4}\\mathbf{p}+\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}=-\\frac{15}{4}\\mathbf{p}+\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{q}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{p}∦\\mathbf{q},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{15}{4}$ and $\\mu =\\frac{4}{3}$"
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186514788,
