          ":question/difficulty": {
            ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/hard"
          },
          ":question/id": "ea6bc20e-dd45-51a3-a105-158a9f1b063a",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/11/1129eb74332cdea7d149aedf6b03abad182ed75d8d5d802ba15ff2503a816c55.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/2e/2e22361f45d4f9c98c2558c298fbcddfda78e205ab2ab39f9ee9dd7a534670a2.png)\n\nIn $△OMR$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OM=\\frac{4}{3}|\\mathbf{q}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OM}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{q}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=-\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $MR=\\frac{11}{3}|\\mathbf{p}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{MR}$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{MR}=\\frac{11}{3}\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MR} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{q}+\\frac{11}{3}\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{3}\\mathbf{p}-\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}=\\frac{11}{3}\\mathbf{p}-\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{q}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{p}∦\\mathbf{q},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =\\frac{11}{3}$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{4}{3}$"
        },
