          ":question/id": "ada3d5e7-07f2-5981-9fcd-59fe51195895",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-120273",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/5d/5dea155246de4f35697780db7ebf1802cdf5c2899fe42fa494993be2f65dd7f3.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{h}=\\lambda \\mathbf{f}+\\mu \\mathbf{g}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu .$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/99/9905448ad59cb2f68f5def832811eb34056305d1a535f23387f4403a340cb8f8.png)\n\nIn $△OFH$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OF=\\frac{1}{2}|\\mathbf{f}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OF}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{f}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OF}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{f}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $FH=2|\\mathbf{g}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{FH}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{g}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{FH}=-2\\mathbf{g}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{h} & =\\overset{\\to }{OF}+\\overset{\\to }{FH} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{f}-2\\mathbf{g}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{h}=\\lambda \\mathbf{f}+\\mu \\mathbf{g}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{f}-2\\mathbf{g}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{f}∦\\mathbf{g},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\mu =-2$"
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186572725,
          ":question/answer-fields": [
            {
              ":answer-field/choices": [
                {
                  ":answer/id": "1e8cd8a3-5848-5337-929a-567d0fe4c28c",
                  ":answer/type": {
                    ":db/ident": ":answer.type/text"
                  },
                  ":answer/value": "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =2$",
                  ":db/id": 17592186572355
                },
                {
                  ":answer/id": "58536380-845e-5010-b39b-f5e39e4c551c",
                  ":answer/type": {
