          ":question/difficulty": {
            ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
          },
          ":question/id": "eeb1828d-7e99-5d6a-aa95-0e286af84c7f",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-120265",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/67/6715bda9432c2ba3e61c31f436cd6b7608312da499bb4372944d25db6cf76090.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mu \\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu .$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/89/8997fda665e4bba248dd19f5b8fe0d2a2524df341042271a0f8426e0ce152ecd.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=3|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=-3\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=2|\\mathbf{b}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=-2\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mu \\mathbf{b}=-3\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$"
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186582353,
          ":question/answer-fields": [
            {
              ":answer-field/choices": [
