          ":question/difficulty": {
            ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
          },
          ":question/id": "1a51e863-8cb5-5ea3-be4b-fcc4293d84e3",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-120275",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/cb/cbd0ffe0fb9e879f2a93557da1aef08622c9e048e14d83c4f4619cf5a1cd47bb.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{w}=\\lambda \\mathbf{u}+\\mu \\mathbf{v}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu .$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/fb/fba4b74fde44c862267cd2c58852f4f829f5b07ead948b0c18d79e94f7d1c0fc.png)\n\nIn $△OUW$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OU=2|\\mathbf{u}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OU}$ and $\\mathbf{u}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=2\\mathbf{u}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $UW=3|\\mathbf{v}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{UW}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{v}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{UW}=-3\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{w} & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{u}-3\\mathbf{v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{w}=\\lambda \\mathbf{u}+\\mu \\mathbf{v}=2\\mathbf{u}-3\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{u}∦\\mathbf{v},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-3$"
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186735007,
          ":question/answer-fields": [
            {
              ":answer-field/choices": [
