          ],
          ":question/difficulty": {
            ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
          },
          ":question/id": "fdf23a75-ec61-5ac9-a21d-3757ff6f8cb1",
          ":question/problem": "The vectors are drawn on a square grid, and $\\mathbf{c}=\\overrightarrow{OC}$.\n\n![Vector diagram on a square grid](images/a4/a4e769a145f41df882458202f1e9540ea17580e3bd661236cd334c8c68478133.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+\\mu\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$\\mu=$ {{field-2}}",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![Completed vector path](images/be/be23bdf90ef85ad1d8b373ca25d9112b6d574c316f729c90db4269bdc3984147.png)\n\nDraw a path $O\\to X\\to C$, first parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$ and then parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$, as shown.\n\nFrom $O$ to $X$, move three squares down. The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ moves two squares up, so $OX=\\frac{3}{2}|\\mathbf{a}|$ and the directions are opposite. Hence $\\overrightarrow{OX}=-\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nFrom $X$ to $C$, move four squares right and two squares up. This is twice the displacement of $\\mathbf{b}$, in the same direction, so $\\overrightarrow{XC}=2\\mathbf{b}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\overrightarrow{OX}+\\overrightarrow{XC}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe two reference vectors are not parallel, so matching coefficients gives $\\lambda=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\mu=2$. The negative sign records the downward part of the route; a length ratio alone does not give its sign."
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186735240,
          ":question/answer-fields": [
            {
              ":answer-field/choices": [
                {
                  ":answer/id": "bafc4198-e4dd-5be3-8eef-1544cf656974",
