          ":question/id": "03cb3efc-e3ab-52b7-b9e6-28cbeee3b50c",
          ":question/problem": "The vectors are drawn on a square grid, and $\\mathbf{c}=\\overrightarrow{OC}$.\n\n![Vector diagram](images/b8/b87f77266a2d44642d01744a5bdcecf42345dd5ffd26531eae5af7c77bea2722.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+\\mu\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$\\mu=$ {{field-2}}",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![Completed vector route](images/90/9013b552fbf1df93e6872f3fd213f1c80e3d4c1e935dd2317f04d52b69d3cdd2.png)\n\nConstruct the route $O\\to X\\to C$, first parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$ and then parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nAlong $OX$, move $12$ squares down. A copy of $\\mathbf{b}$ moves $4$ squares up. Thus $OX=3|\\mathbf{b}|$ and the segment points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{b}$. Hence $\\overrightarrow{OX}=-3\\mathbf{b}$.\n\nAlong $XC$, move $3$ squares left and $3$ squares up. A copy of $\\mathbf{a}$ moves $2$ squares left and $2$ squares up. Thus $XC=\\frac{3}{2}|\\mathbf{a}|$ and the segment points in the same direction to $\\mathbf{a}$. Hence $\\overrightarrow{XC}=\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{a}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\overrightarrow{OX}+\\overrightarrow{XC}\\\\&=-3\\mathbf{b}+\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{a}\\\\&=\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReorder the terms to match their vector names. Since $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, their coefficients are unique. Matching coefficients gives $\\lambda=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\mu=-3$."
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186735249,
          ":question/answer-fields": [
