          ":question/id": "628e7c86-511a-5a96-a42d-cd88be4d4559",
          ":question/problem": "The vectors are drawn on a square grid, and $\\mathbf{c}=\\overrightarrow{OC}$.\n\n![Vector diagram](images/6f/6f39fa0f0006ca782883fcd6ce5f5f687e8ec90cceb15d1cc5eda21cd0ba4c14.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+\\mu\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$\\mu=$ {{field-2}}",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![Completed vector route](images/62/62405de33e197db1e8ef48b2d39c5909179e00c9b933cb8b2820101f0323cbb9.png)\n\nConstruct the route $O\\to X\\to C$, first parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$ and then parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nAlong $OX$, move $6$ squares left and $6$ squares down. A copy of $\\mathbf{b}$ moves $2$ squares right and $2$ squares up. Thus $OX=3|\\mathbf{b}|$ and the segment points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{b}$. Hence $\\overrightarrow{OX}=-3\\mathbf{b}$.\n\nAlong $XC$, move $1$ square up. A copy of $\\mathbf{a}$ moves $1$ square up. Thus $XC=1|\\mathbf{a}|$ and the segment points in the same direction to $\\mathbf{a}$. Hence $\\overrightarrow{XC}=\\mathbf{a}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\overrightarrow{OX}+\\overrightarrow{XC}\\\\&=-3\\mathbf{b}+\\mathbf{a}\\\\&=\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReorder the terms to match their vector names. Since $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, their coefficients are unique. Matching coefficients gives $\\lambda=1$ and $\\mu=-3$."
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186735252,
