          },
          ":question/id": "6ccc521b-cd0f-55f5-8aee-f604b35684c4",
          ":question/problem": "The vectors are drawn on a square grid, and $\\mathbf{c}=\\overrightarrow{OC}$.\n\n![Vector diagram](images/92/9237b017099fc15166673e8f9845d5aef871761499574352d736d3c0dd7da37b.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+\\mu\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$\\mu=$ {{field-2}}",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![Completed vector route](images/e9/e97aaf366a2e97335af9df979f71d5123badc630a6d35e24d839e52e1124e671.png)\n\nConstruct the route $O\\to X\\to C$, first parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$ and then parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$.\n\nAlong $OX$, move $4$ squares right. A copy of $\\mathbf{a}$ moves $1$ square right. Thus $OX=4|\\mathbf{a}|$ and the segment points in the same direction to $\\mathbf{a}$. Hence $\\overrightarrow{OX}=4\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nAlong $XC$, move $4$ squares right and $8$ squares down. A copy of $\\mathbf{b}$ moves $1$ square right and $2$ squares down. Thus $XC=4|\\mathbf{b}|$ and the segment points in the same direction to $\\mathbf{b}$. Hence $\\overrightarrow{XC}=4\\mathbf{b}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\overrightarrow{OX}+\\overrightarrow{XC}\\\\&=4\\mathbf{a}+4\\mathbf{b}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, their coefficients are unique. Matching coefficients gives $\\lambda=4$ and $\\mu=4$."
        }
      ],
      ":knowledge-point/title": "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"
    },
    "17592186096653": {
      ":db/id": 17592186096653,
      ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
        ":db/id": 17592186096452,
        ":question/id": "09d593cc-526e-5691-9a18-efff515eae59",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "e-4288",
        ":question/problem": "Consider the following diagram, where $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b},$ and $X$ divides $AB$ in the ratio $3:1.$ Find $\\overrightarrow{OX}.$\n\n![](images/67/678ea2936b8a5b1ded74cb8a0875c629e8be0f7002c98492ebf2e8109fc603ef.png)",
        ":question/worked-solution": "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a},$ so we just need to work out $\\overrightarrow{AX}.$\n\nFirst, from $\\triangle OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AX:XB=3:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AX}=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\dfrac{3}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OX} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AX} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{3}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{3}{4}\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
      ":knowledge-point/id": "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad",
      ":knowledge-point/level-coverage": [
        {
          ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
        }
      ],
      ":knowledge-point/question-generator": {
