          ":question/id": "c29ac42f-42ba-5668-9b76-4de9b80fc725",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-120303",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/50/50b87e86bd2985e54ce0a8fe6b3e23986f7e1946a5284d0ae297d96b9cbc60f6.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=\\mathbf{m},$ $\\overset{\\to }{ON}=\\mathbf{n}$ and $P$ divides $\\overline{MN}$ in the ratio $MP:PN=2:1.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OP}.$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MP}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=\\mathbf{m},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{MP}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OMN,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MN} & =\\overset{\\to }{MO}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{m}+\\mathbf{n} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $MP:PN=2:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{MP}=\\frac{2}{3}\\overset{\\to }{MN}=\\frac{2}{3}(\\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{m}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OP} & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MP} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{m}+\\frac{2}{3}(\\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{m}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{m}+\\frac{2}{3}\\mathbf{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
        },
