          ":question/problem": "![](images/d9/d9448f9f05666f9806975b37a73f09c492eb73139b6f9395b619f077f3a584c9.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\mathbf{p},$ $\\overset{\\to }{OQ}=\\mathbf{q},$ and $R$ divides $\\overline{PQ}$ in the ratio $PR:RQ=4:9.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ via the vectors $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OR}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mathbf{p}+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\mathbf{q}$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OR}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\mathbf{p},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{PR}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OPQ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{PO}+\\overset{\\to }{OQ} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{OQ} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{q}-\\mathbf{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $PR:RQ=4:9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{PR}=\\frac{4}{13}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}=\\frac{4}{13}(\\mathbf{q}-\\mathbf{p}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OR} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{p}+\\frac{4}{13}(\\mathbf{q}-\\mathbf{p}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{13}\\mathbf{p}+\\frac{4}{13}\\mathbf{q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
        },
