          ":question/difficulty": {
            ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/hard"
          },
          ":question/id": "3125360d-8b47-5e6c-bbea-812b2836b4ce",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/d5/d58ca71ebfed4f3046819076ea6fc74da297e3e874188327de4753f9e3e1e154.png)\n\nIn the diagram, $X$ lies on the segment $AB$, with lengths $AX:XB=1:2$. The given vectors point toward $O$: $\\overrightarrow{AO}=a$ and $\\overrightarrow{BO}=b$.\n\nExpress $\\overrightarrow{OX}$ in terms of $a$ and $b$ by filling in the two coefficients, including their signs.\n\n$\\overrightarrow{OX}=$ {{field-1}} $a+$ {{field-2}} $b$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The known vectors point toward $O$, so their reverses are\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA}=-a,\\qquad \\overrightarrow{OB}=-b.\n$$\nThe path $A\\to O\\to B$ gives\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\overrightarrow{AO}+\\overrightarrow{OB}=a-b.\n$$\nThe ratio $AX:XB=1:2$ means\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AX}=\\frac{1}{3}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\frac{1}{3}(a-b).\n$$\nNow trace $O\\to A\\to X$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{OX}&=\\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}\\\\\n&=-a+\\frac{1}{3}(a-b)\\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{3}a-\\frac{1}{3}b.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe two coefficients are $-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $-\\frac{1}{3}$. Both are negative because the given vectors run toward $O$, whereas $\\overrightarrow{OX}$ runs away from $O$ into the triangle."
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186734990,
