          ":question/difficulty": {
            ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
          },
          ":question/id": "348155ce-098a-5161-8b84-34cfe39ea95e",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/c1/c1ba013c40d2885305902a386c05c7a2080048537fd3d1d40e94cb367a4d8a62.png)\n\nIn the diagram, $P$ lies on the segment $VE$. The given vectors are $\\overrightarrow{OV}=p$ and $\\overrightarrow{OE}=b$. The lengths satisfy $VP:PE=3:11$.\n\nExpress $\\overrightarrow{OP}$ in terms of $p$ and $b$.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "First express both endpoint vectors in the directions away from $O$:\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OV}=p\\qquad \\overrightarrow{OE}=b .\n$$\n\nTrace the path from $V$ through $O$ to $E$:\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{VE}=\\overrightarrow{VO}+\\overrightarrow{OE}=-p+b.\n$$\n\nThe ratio $VP:PE=3:11$ gives $14$ parts in the whole segment. Thus\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{VP}=\\frac{3}{14}\\overrightarrow{VE}=\\frac{3}{14}(-p+b).\n$$\n\nAdd along the path $O\\to V\\to P$ and collect coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overrightarrow{OP}&=\\overrightarrow{OV}+\\overrightarrow{VP}\\\\&=p+\\frac{3}{14}(-p+b)\\\\&=\\frac{11}{14}p+\\frac{3}{14}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186735291,
          ":question/answer-fields": [
