          ":question/id": "7a4f66a0-1cfd-52d9-be40-ea47c3b77cf3",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-20435",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/4b/4b7c8c679171c1dcb6ef44b33696ba2ccce87b3f690643af979476d8dfb285ce.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda .$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $3\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/f6/f67af4512a7ab61f862932a60dafc25ef62bc46335b7d7df323fe889f8728e6b.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3\\mathbf{b}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{2}|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{b}+\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}=\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$"
        },
