          ":question/id": "1d2b32e7-2ad4-5f8a-a95d-8e55e6774201",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-70140",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/75/7578bb2e5b594161744d96d918fa75e0225e723138d010dba3ed635098e75ca2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b},$ find $\\lambda .$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $\\mathbf{a}.$ So, we begin with the vector $\\mathbf{a},$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/47/4797ffa54b3c2e4d177a6c3d527b1aaf3f1460d3c509bca86395e5f360314d25.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=3|\\mathbf{b}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=3\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{b},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3\n$$"
        },
