          ":question/id": "1a97697a-0ce5-5dbb-bf9f-1e792385b4e9",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-70187",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/24/245a7b9af068b057e16a0c84971f5bb356a9fc56374cb3c17c4896d7a9b815ca.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b},$ find $\\lambda .$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we begin with the vector $\\mathbf{b},$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/9b/9b99ad62bfedf6945020d345ef0bbc3531c58af1088698a78daf482cd4c14fe1.png)\n\nNote that in $△OBC,$ we have:\n\n- $BC=4|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{BC}=4\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{b}+4\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{b}+4\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =4\n$$"
        },
