          ":question/id": "049a751e-712d-54cc-ad6d-c6e3564444d6",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-120247",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/2e/2ea6acbf6574e970a798100729de9a92874bca90b8153db9883daa1cd6d98c2a.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda .$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $2\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/3c/3c31e9b0d926de6a3f8c9f35bd816349d6488c8b98864d8576fdbfc395bbd92d.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2\\mathbf{b}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=3\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{b}+3\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}=3\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3\n$$"
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186572695,
          ":question/answer-fields": [
            {
              ":answer-field/choices": [
                {
                  ":answer/id": "57534557-824c-57ce-9724-420aeeed76a9",
