          ],
          ":question/difficulty": {
            ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
          },
          ":question/id": "66d86028-4215-5205-b200-ed09b51ad511",
          ":question/problem": "The arrows show $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf a$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf b$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf c$ on a square grid.\n\n![](images/e7/e781601d7f485104824be4324946dfb9e7adadc23dac64986a35d438f5428aec.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf c=\\lambda\\mathbf a+2\\mathbf b$, find $\\lambda$.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "First draw $2\\mathbf b$ from $O$, and call its endpoint $P$. Since $\\mathbf b$ goes one square left and two squares up, $2\\mathbf b$ goes two squares left and four squares up.\n\n![](images/81/817a1572fd17f7924f7c4808f04b273ed0ab8ed852368f0f911b40d9b3458b84.png)\n\nThe remaining vector $\\overrightarrow{PC}$ goes six squares right. It has the same direction as $\\mathbf a$, which goes four squares right. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PC}=\\frac{6}{4}\\mathbf a=\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf a.\n$$\n\nUsing the path from $O$ through $P$ to $C$,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf c=\\overrightarrow{OP}+\\overrightarrow{PC}=2\\mathbf b+\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf a.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$ are not parallel, matching their unique coefficients with $\\mathbf c=\\lambda\\mathbf a+2\\mathbf b$ gives $\\lambda=\\frac{3}{2}$."
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186735038,
          ":question/answer-fields": [
            {
              ":answer-field/choices": [
                {
                  ":answer/id": "7651a6ea-aff0-509f-bfc5-a375145825d8",
                  ":answer/type": {
                    ":db/ident": ":answer.type/math"
                  },
                  ":answer/value": "\\frac{3}{4}",
                  ":db/id": 17592186735033
                }
              ],
              ":answer-field/correct": {
                ":answer/id": "7651a6ea-aff0-509f-bfc5-a375145825d8",
                ":answer/type": {
                  ":db/ident": ":answer.type/math"
                },
                ":answer/value": "\\frac{3}{4}",
                ":db/id": 17592186735033
              },
              ":answer-field/id": "82083fa6-8670-5d7b-b48c-3d51731bdad6",
              ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
              ":answer-field/type": {
                ":db/ident": ":answer-field.type/blank"
              },
              ":db/id": 17592186735034
            }
          ],
          ":question/difficulty": {
            ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/hard"
          },
          ":question/id": "e4997348-10cf-5382-8f15-1b6111ade6f2",
          ":question/problem": "The arrows show $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf a$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf b$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf c$. Solid grid lines are one unit apart; dotted lines divide each square in half.\n\n![](images/48/485299e056823946fcd349f9cab69b45d12127237a192e2869e322e744ed182f.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf c=\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf a+\\lambda\\mathbf b$, find $\\lambda$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Construct the known component first. The vector $\\mathbf a$ goes three squares right and two squares up, so $\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf a$ goes $4\\frac{1}{2}$ squares right and three squares up. Let $P$ be its endpoint.\n\n![](images/27/27fce7c173b5408cedf468544984f9d779c9b8b608569f530dc0f20e71f1ac06.png)\n\nFrom $P$ to $C$, the remaining displacement is $1\\frac{1}{2}$ squares right and three squares up. Compare this with $\\mathbf b$, which goes two squares right and four squares up:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Horizontal scale} &= \\frac{\\frac{3}{2}}{2}=\\frac{3}{4}, \\\\ \\text{Vertical scale} &= \\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth grid displacements have the same positive scale factor. Thus $\\overrightarrow{PC}$ has the same direction as $\\mathbf b$ and three quarters of its length:\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PC}=\\frac{3}{4}\\mathbf b.\n$$\n\nThe triangle law now gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf c=\\overrightarrow{OP}+\\overrightarrow{PC}=\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf a+\\frac{3}{4}\\mathbf b.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$ are not parallel, these coefficients are unique. Matching the coefficient of $\\mathbf b$ gives $\\lambda=\\frac{3}{4}$."
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186735040,
          ":question/answer-fields": [
            {
              ":answer-field/choices": [
                {
                  ":answer/id": "432a506a-ffc9-5003-92e4-33077e6c36cf",
                  ":answer/type": {
                    ":db/ident": ":answer.type/math"
                  },
                  ":answer/value": "3",
                  ":db/id": 17592186735029
                },
                {
