            ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/easy"
          },
          ":question/id": "b085101b-b235-5f7f-b623-4ac3e21615ca",
          ":question/problem": "The arrows show $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf a$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf b$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf c$. Grid squares have side length one unit.\n\n![](images/fa/fa4ee2495b26b12cb8b8c0ad7f3bcf6713067ef2bf7b6f2d4c3bca9af7b97af1.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Construct the known component $\\mathbf{a}$ from $O$, ending at $A$. Count signed horizontal and vertical grid displacements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{OA} &=1\\,(-5, 0)=(-5, 0). \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{AC} &=(0, 6).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/fa/fae0f9f3a7c91dd9aa15bedf707bd50893dc6fff510677f515a9b74250be24f8.png)\n\nCompare the remaining displacement with $\\mathbf{b}$, whose grid displacement is $(0, 4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Vertical scale} &=\\frac{6}{4}=\\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scale is positive, so the remaining vector has the same direction as $\\mathbf{b}$. Thus $\\overrightarrow{AC}=\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{b}$. By the triangle law,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c} &=\\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AC} \\\\\n&=\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{b} \\\\\n&=\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, so their coefficients are unique. Matching the coefficient of $\\mathbf{b}$ gives $\\lambda=\\frac{3}{2}$."
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186735395,
          ":question/answer-fields": [
